<p class="wp-block-paragraph">This week&#8217;s sponsor, <a href="https://cotypist.app/macsparky">Cotypist, helps you keep writing on your Mac</a>. It suggests the next few words right where you&#8217;re typing. Take what you want with Tab, or keep typing when you have something else in mind.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">I&#8217;ve written about Cotypist&#8217;s autocomplete before. This time, there&#8217;s another part of the app worth a look: help finding a word, whether you&#8217;re halfway through a sentence or going back over a draft.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Word Alternatives gives you a few possible next words when you pause. Turn it on in Cotypist Labs, and a numbered list appears below the cursor. If you&#8217;d rather ask for suggestions than have them appear whenever you stop, you can assign a shortcut instead.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">That distinction matters. Pausing to think doesn&#8217;t always mean you want help. I like having a choice about when the app offers it.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The second feature works on words you&#8217;ve already written. Select a word or short phrase, and Cotypist suggests synonyms that fit the surrounding sentence. Press the corresponding number to replace your selection. You can change when those suggestions appear, too.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">For example, you might have the right meaning in an email but want a less formal word. Or you might notice the same adjective twice in a paragraph. A short list of alternatives gives you something to consider while the sentence is still in front of you. You decide whether any of them actually improves it.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Both features are part of Pro. The suggestions are generated on your Mac, so your writing doesn&#8217;t need to go to a server for that work. Cotypist runs on Apple Silicon Macs with macOS 14 or later and works in most Mac apps.</p>



